ts that  my staff is currently working on  to reconcile and determine which should  be count and which are ineligible to be  counted  first is the haver provisional and i  think dan also gave you some information  from our website describing exactly what  i have a provisional is but basically  it's  someone who reported that they were  registered to vote however they were not  in the voter registration system when  they came in to vote  so they voted a provisional ballot and  we have to do research to see if there's  something that we've done  that didn't get correctly done and they  should be registered but if they're not  registered then the votes won't be  eligible to be counted  we also have the same day registration  and the acronyms are i  o and u the difference between those is  that  the same day registration in person is  somebody who showed up at an early  voting or election day  site provided the nevada id that's  required by state law  and they were registered to vote on the  spot and given a ballot to vote  the same day registration 0 represents  the online  registration and voters were eligible to  do that until  october 29th the thursday before  election day  to register online which also requires  uh nevada id in order to go on and vote  the sdru is the simplest of all of them  it's an update  so as a result of changes in state law  voters can come in and on the spot  during early voting or election day  change their address  change their democratic party and  different items  so those are the three different types  in addition to the hava and my staff is  currently working  there's a fairly large group working on  verifying going through those because  the next step  and i have communicated with the  secretary of state's office  and they have confirmed that they're  going to be asking us for our first  report  on all voters who voted in clark county  individually  whether it be early voting election day  or through the mail  to them on monday and what the secretary  of state needs to do with that  is compare it with the other 16 counties  in the state of nevada  to make sure that we don't have any  duplicates in those that we voted  provisionally or that anybody has done  anything illegal  by voting in two different counties once  they run that review  they'll send that information back to us  on anybody who  ballot shouldn't be counted and will  begin to  in an automated fashion begin to roll in  those provisional votes which probably  won't be  any earlier than wednesday of next week  so keep in mind despite the mail ballot  ballots that we're putting through in  reading  we won't even have the capability to  enter these sixty thousand  which not all of those will count  however process any of those until any  earlier than wednesday of this week  so the grand total on the breakdown for  those provisional ballots is there's  twenty one thousand four hundred and  ninety democrats nineteen thousand  ten nonpartisan ballots and  nineteen thousand nine hundred seven  republican provisional ballots  okay in the report on u.s mail for this  morning we received  25 that's two five  uh ballots through the mail this morning  and then the process of being  reviewed and sent to count if  they are eligible and i've already  mentioned but just to close out  the cure process for any voter in clark  county  the deadline is this thursday the 12th  of november  and again the hotline number for  assistance on that is 702-455-6552  and with that i'll open it up for any  questions  thank you good morning um two for you on  the um the id deadline was yesterday  you said you're working on pulling those  do you have a number there were 44 000 i  believe outstanding that had an id issue  do you know how many were cleared  unfortunately i don't know exactly how  many of those records i can tell you  that we have a large group of staff  that's going through those to identify  them and start pulling the ballots  okay those voters can be counted i  possibly might have one  update for that for tomorrow but no  later than monday okay and just to  clarify the numbers that you gave us off  the top  um you said a total of 23 000 have been  counted  yes but that's 23 000 since when since  we've been talking about this universal  ballots and that 63 63.00063  23 000. it's 234.89  got it and then 39 000 left that is  correct perfect pretty closely adds up  to 63. and then just confirming one last  thing on these um  on the sdrs so you'll send that first  report  monday that's where we've been  instructed and then when you get that  then back on wednesday  the number whatever the secretary of  state tells you are eligible  how long do you think it will take you  to process those it is an automated  process  but it depends on what time in the day  we get them on wednesday  but we should be able to roll them into  the system if we receive it in the  morning  otherwise it would be on thursday which  is our last day statutorily  to get all the ballots in  good morning mr gloria i have a couple  of questions one morning  one on uh will you be open saturday and  sunday for curing  for people who yes sir the the hotline  is open the the  number that i advertise 702-455-6552  i want to ask about security there was  another protest last night  and others are planned we understand  have you heard or experienced any  credible threats  to your facilities or the count process  and that's a security issue and it's  more a question for law enforcement to  respond to  not that i know of can i ask how you  feel  fantastic would that be a  feeling of relief i don't think it's a  feeling a relief we're continuing to  roll through we're very proud of the  work that we've done and the dedication  from staff  we feel good because we're continuing to  report the work has not stopped  despite everything that's going on  around us so we've got a brave set of  workers who've been coming to work  despite what's going on around us but  that is because law enforcement and our  county management has done an  excellent job of putting the protection  in place for all of us to feel safe  coming to work and doing what we need to  do  over here  morning mr gloria you're experienced  professional could you  explain to us why almost 100 of the late  ballots  across the battleground states went for  joe biden  have you ever seen a situation like this  during the prior elections  thank you so much i'm not really  qualified to answer that you need to  talk to some journalists from cbs  and cnn and all that kind of thing i i  don't get into the details  of why valid shift or why count shift we  count ballots as they come in so we  don't analyze why it would ship one way  or the other  hi joe good morning can you break down  the timeline of when those  mail-in ballots that were walked up and  dropped off in person  actually first started being counted the  election day ones  so the election day drop-offs your  question is just to make sure that i  understand it  when did we start putting them through  the process correct  i believe those started going in through  the agilis machine sometime on thursday  afternoon but no later than friday  morning  what was the delay there because we  needed to get the computers that were  used on election day  the update that you had described on  wednesday started  that was certainly important i think  i've mentioned in my previous conference  that it's really important that we get  the database  updated with those laptops that were in  the system  so that we can have the computer do the  important work of kicking those ballots  out  for anybody who voted in person they  shouldn't have sent that ballot in but  we have to make sure that we don't get  those ballots in the system to be  counted so  yes that would have been one reason but  we were still able to read them in  and those ballots were um posted  and in queue until we got all the data  in over here  hi joe thanks for taking all of our  questions i'm gonna move away from the  speaker i just have two quick questions  for you first  in this sort of second half of the count  going towards  november 12th november 16th oh i'm sorry  i was just wondering you know how does  it feel in terms of how it's going now  versus earlier in this week what have  you guys  learned in this process  well in terms of how it feels i think  consistently through we've been doing  everything we can to keep the process  going  i can't nail anything down that  specifically slowed us down  i think it's very important that  everybody understands  mail for the entire voting group here  we became a mail ballot county in march  of this year  and so there's a lot of expectation from  people around the country and also from  the media  that we be as good at processing male  ballots as colorado oregon or washington  who have been doing it for 12 or 13  years  we simply don't have the equipment the  facilities  the staff that have been trained in that  area to keep up with the pace that they  can put up where they normally deal with  male ballots  but we've done a very good job of  accommodating that we got equipment  with help from the federal government  from the secretary of state who's been  very supportive in everything that we do  and our local government here in clark  county  we knew the equipment that we needed we  implemented  exactly what we could according to the  budget and  we got ourselves in a good position to  put us right now in a great position to  finish on time  also with the special session this  summer  they gave us additional days to count  that was critical to us being in the  position that we're in right now  had they not given us days prior to  election day and an extension  we wouldn't be where we are right now so  legislators  all of the political figures here in  nevada did a great job of listening to  election officials and us telling them  this is what we need  in order for us to properly process  ballots so  that that's where i feel we stand thank  you i just have one quick follow-up for  you  what is your reaction to the projections  from multiple news organizations that  joe biden is the president-elect  i don't really have any reaction we  we've got our work to do here we can't  stop what we're doing  um i feel good for those election  administrators who where the calls goes  in you obviously  feel good but that call has not been  made for nevada so  we're still working as hard as we can to  get things completed so thank you for  those questions  next  i'm sorry to ask you more questions  about numbers but do you have the  partisan breakdown on the 34  743 drop off mail ballots  oh i see what you're saying from the  election day dropping the election do we  know how many are democrats  i don't have those broken down i have  the total breakdown well no i don't have  those broken down  and then the uh the 44 000 ballots that  needed the id cure or  id was needed was that included in the  provisional ballots or is that a  separate universe of ballots  there are a lot of ballots that are in  that group it could be  the online ballots the mail ballots so  they're different groups that require an  id that  didn't provide okay so they are spread  across those ballots  and then yesterday the federalists  reported that a man who died in 2017  named fred stoke jr had his ballot  returned uh are you or the secretary of  state's office investigating  i'm not aware of that case but we  certainly will if it's brought to my  attention  and do you compare your voter rolls to  the social security death index  we have several reports that come  through from several entities to clean  up our voter roles  hi thank you for taking these questions  you had mentioned additional equipment  and staffing capabilities that were  aided you from the state and the  locality can you talk a little bit more  about when you got that additional  capacity and what it looks like  well starting in march when we became  aware of us being  having the need to send out bail ballots  to all voters as  directed by the secretary of state we  immediately began work  in contacting our vendor or a proposed  vendor for  our mail ballot processing machine which  has made a huge  impact on our ability to count ballots  so the agilis machine  came in uh it obviously it took some  time to get it delivered and get it  implemented we  we use it in the primary election uh we  continue to use it now for the general  election  we immediately well the in-person voting  in the primary was very different than  what we provided  for the general there was only uh one  early voting site and three election day  sites but moving to the general  immediately in june we started working  with risk management to develop a plan  on exactly what we do to set up sites so  they were safe for voters and our  workers  and there was an increase in funding to  us because we needed to buy  shields to protect the voter from the  clerk we needed to buy ppe we needed to  buy cleanser  another outstanding group who has  supported us the mac  our fire chief here steinbeck has done  an excellent job of not only serving the  election department but the entire  county  with what we needed for ppe so that was  a big scale up  and then of course the personnel my  staff did an excellent job of  identifying personnel that we would need  in every area so that we could increase  that we got an increase in funding we  used a  much larger amount of agency temporary  employees because we were unable to  hire what we needed through our normal  process because  in the past a lot of our workers are  senior citizens and they love doing that  work and they do an excellent job of  doing it but  they're more susceptible to the pandemic  so god bless them they just weren't  comfortable coming and working in large  groups so  i think those were areas where we got  the resources that we definitely needed  